    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Archaeological evidence places the carving between 2600 and 2500 BC, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a> or his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>, though no contemporary inscription names its maker.</li><li>Its body was cut from the quarry bedrock of the plateau, and the stone taken from around it built a temple in front that was never finished.</li><li>Drifting sand buried it again and again, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>, Roman officials and modern excavators cleared it in turn.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Its nose was not shot off by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops. The face shows marks of rods or chisels hammered in at the bridge and below the nostril, and the nose has been documented as damaged since at least the 10th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

